<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Murcia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Murcia is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> that formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> during the medieval Islamic period. Its irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huerta">huertas</a>, sustained by hydraulic techniques inherited and developed by Arab cultivators, produced substantial agricultural surpluses. These irrigated lands contributed to the Andalusian economic base of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/murcia.jpg" alt="Murcia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: nesimo · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A1bado_Santo_en_Murcia_19-04-2014_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
